@[system]

※※ What every program starts with, and the devices it starts with open.
※
※ One module for now, named `std` and found beside the compiler.  What a program
※ names here is a *descriptor* -- something that may be read, or written, or
※ both -- and never the machinery underneath it.
※
※ The machinery is the runtime the compiler carries: C, compiled ahead of time
※ for every architecture, reached through `@[external]` and handed the ring as a
※ record laid out the way it reads one.  That is `io`'s, which this module hands
※ on with the devices that carry where the ring is; `fs`, handed on too, keeps
※ the ring and the directories files are opened in.  That there is a ring, and that there is sometimes not one, is
※ not something a program can see.

※※ Files, directories, and what a program does its I/O with: `std.fs`.
@[export]
let fs := ⎕import("fs")

※※ The devices a program reads and writes, and the ring their requests go
※※ through: `std.io`.
@[export]
let io := ⎕import("io")


※※ The devices a program starts with open.
※※
※※ \details Each is `mut`: it may be lent by `&mut` and written where the record
※※ holding it may not -- which is what lets a startup function written
※※ `init: std.Init` hand `&mut init.io.output` to whatever writes.  Writing to one
※※ takes `&mut`, and a second `&mut` to the same place is refused, so two names for
※※ one device is something the compiler refuses rather than something a lock
※※ prevents.  There is no other way to them: a function that writes is handed the
※※ device it writes to.
@[export]
type Io = mut input : io.Reader ; mut output : io.Writer ; mut error : io.Writer

※※ What a program is started with: the devices it inherited, what it does its
※※ I/O with, and the words it was named with.
※※
※※ \details `fs` holds the ring every request goes through.  It is `mut`, so
※※ `&mut init.fs` may be lent to `run` and `spawn`, and it needs to be lent to
※※ nothing else: `std.fs.cwd()` answers the directory the process is in.
※
※ A record rather than the two of them outright, so that whatever else a program
※ inherits has somewhere to go without changing the one signature every program
※ writes.  The environment is not one of those: it is a table and not a run of
※ words, so it is `⎕environ` and the compiler builds it.
※
※ `args` is what the kernel put on the stack, counted: the first is the name the
※ program was run under, as it is everywhere, and the rest are what followed it.
※ Each is a `str`, which is bytes and a length -- the kernel's own shape is a
※ nul at the end, and the length is counted once before the program starts
※ rather than by everything that reads one.
@[export, unique]
type Init = io : Io ; mut fs : fs.State ; args : str⟦⟧

※※ What the kernel said, where it refused.
※
※ The numbers are the kernel's own, so a reader who knows what `EAGAIN` means
※ knows what `would_block` is; the names are the language's, so a reader who
※ does not need not learn them.  `other` is what anything this list does not
※ name comes to -- honest about what it cannot say rather than pretending the
※ list is closed.
※
※ What is named is what a read or a write can actually give, and what opening a
※ file by name can: `fs.Dir.open` reaches the refusals that belong to a path, and
※ `read_lines` refuses a line longer than it holds with `ENOBUFS`.
@[export]
enum Error:
    other = 0 ;
    not_found = 2 ;          ※ ENOENT
    interrupted = 4 ;        ※ EINTR
    device = 5 ;             ※ EIO
    not_a_device = 9 ;       ※ EBADF
    would_block = 11 ;       ※ EAGAIN
    no_memory = 12 ;         ※ ENOMEM
    not_allowed = 13 ;       ※ EACCES
    bad_address = 14 ;       ※ EFAULT
    not_a_directory = 20 ;   ※ ENOTDIR
    is_a_directory = 21 ;    ※ EISDIR
    invalid = 22 ;           ※ EINVAL
    too_many_open = 24 ;     ※ EMFILE
    too_big = 27 ;           ※ EFBIG
    no_room = 28 ;           ※ ENOSPC
    read_only = 30 ;         ※ EROFS
    broken_pipe = 32 ;       ※ EPIPE
    name_too_long = 36 ;     ※ ENAMETOOLONG
    link_loop = 40 ;         ※ ELOOP
    reset = 104 ;            ※ ECONNRESET
    no_buffer = 105          ※ ENOBUFS

※※ What the kernel answered, read as one of those.
※
※ The enumeration's numbers are the kernel's, so this is the question "is this
※ number one of them" and nothing else -- which is what narrowing to an
※ enumeration asks.  Anything it does not name is `other`.
※
※ What `read`, `write` and `flush` carry is the number itself, which cannot be
※ wrong; this is here for a program that would rather compare against a name.
@[export]
fn as_error(n: i32) → Error:
    ⎕narrow(n, ⌜Error⌝) ?? Error.other

※※ Each `Error` with its name, as the enumeration writes it.
※
※ Run while compiling to lay out `error_names`: the names are read off the
※ enumeration, so a value added to `Error` is named with nothing else to write.
fn named_errors() → ⸨Error: str⸩:
    let names: mut ⸨Error: str⸩ = ⸨⸩
    comptime foreach one := ⎕fields(⌜Error⌝):
        names⸨one⟦1⟧⸩ ← one⟦0⟧
    names

※※ The name of each `Error`, in the image.
let error_names: ⸨Error: str⸩ = named_errors() in ⎕static

※※ The name of an `Error`, as the enumeration writes it.
※※
※※ \param e the value
※※ \return its name: `not_allowed` for `Error.not_allowed`
@[export]
fn error_name_of(e: Error) → str:
    error_names⸨e⸩ ?? "other"

※※ The name of what the kernel answered, as `Error` writes it.
※※
※※ \param code the number the kernel gave -- what `read`, `write`, `flush` and
※※ `fs.Dir.open` carry when they fail
※※ \return the name of the `Error` it is: `not_allowed` for 13, and `other` for
※※ a number the enumeration does not name
※
※ A read of a table in the image, so it is pure: nothing is filled in, and a
※ program that never fails carries the table and does nothing with it.
@[export]
fn error_name(code: i32) → str:
    error_name_of(as_error(code))

※ -- reading and writing --------------------------------------------------------
※
※ Every device carries where the ring is, so nothing here names a variable at the
※ top level: what a request goes through is what the caller handed over.

※※ What the kernel said, where it refused, as it says it: a positive number,
※※ which is the number `⎕errno` would have been.
fn refusal(of: i64) → i32:
    ⎕narrow(0i64 - of, ⌜i32⌝) ?? 0i32

※※ Wait for everything outstanding.  The entry point does this when the startup
※※ function has returned; nothing stops a program doing it sooner.
@[export]
fn drain(s: &mut fs.State):
    fs.State.drain(s)

※※ Start writing `what` to `to`, answering the request that was started.
※
※ It does not wait: the kernel is told about the request when something waits
※ for it, which is `flush` or the drain a program does before it ends.  So
※ several writes may be in flight at once, and a program that wants to know one
※ of them happened says so.
※
※ The kernel reads a run of bytes, so a view that is not one -- a column, every
※ other byte -- is copied into one first, in `⎕heap`.  The kernel reads it after
※ this has returned, so the request keeps it, and it is given back when the
※ kernel's answer comes in.
@[export]
fn write(to: &mut io.Writer, what: u8⟦⟧) → io.Pending ? i32:
    let laid: u8⟦⟧ = ⎕one_run(what)
    let at: u64 = if #laid = 0: 0u64 else: ⎕address(&laid⟦0⟧)
    if #laid ≠ 0 and ⎕address(&laid⟦0⟧) ≠ ⎕address(&what⟦0⟧):
        io.Writer.start_kept(to, at, ⎕drop(#laid))
    else:
        io.Writer.start(to, at, ⎕drop(#laid))

※※ Wait for a request and answer how many bytes went, or what the kernel said.
@[export]
fn flush(of: io.Pending) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    let got: i64 = io.Pending.wait(of)
    if got < 0i64:
        ⊥ refusal(got)
    else:
        ⎕unit(⎕narrow(got, ⌜u64⌝) ?? 0u64, ⌜size⌝)

※※ Write `what` to `to` and wait for it, answering how many bytes went.
※
※ `write` and `flush` in one, which is what a program that has nothing else to
※ do while it waits should say.
@[export]
fn write_sync(to: &mut io.Writer, what: u8⟦⟧) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    flush(write(to, what)?)

※※ What an answer says: how many bytes went, or what the kernel refused with --
※※ what `flush` answers for the same request.
@[export]
fn done(of: io.Answer) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    if of.got < 0i64:
        ⊥ refusal(of.got)
    else:
        ⎕unit(⎕narrow(of.got, ⌜u64⌝) ?? 0u64, ⌜size⌝)

※※ Start reading up to `len` bytes from `from`, for a task to wait for.
※※
※※ \param from the device to read
※※ \param len how many bytes to ask for at most
※※ \return the request, which the task hands `⎕await`
※※ \raises the number the kernel gave, or `EAGAIN` where every slot is taken
※
※ The bytes go into room the ring keeps rather than into the task, whose frame
※ the kernel would otherwise be writing into while the task does something else;
※ `received` hands them over as a list of the task's own once it has the answer.
@[export]
fn read_start(from: &mut io.Reader, len: u64) → io.Pending ? i32:
    io.Reader.start_kept(from, len)

※※ What a read for a task brought: the bytes, as a list of the task's own.
※※
※※ \param of what `⎕await` answered for the request `read_start` started
※※ \return the bytes that came, none where there is no more to come
※※ \raises the number the kernel gave
@[export]
fn received(of: io.Answer) → [u8] ? i32:
    if of.got < 0i64:
        ⊥ refusal(of.got)
    elif of.at = 0u64:
        []
    else:
        ⎕span(of.at, ⎕narrow(of.got, ⌜u64⌝) ?? 0u64, ⌜[u8]⌝)

※※ Hand a task to `run`.
※※
※※ \param s what the program does its I/O with, which keeps the tasks: `&mut init.fs`
※※ \param walk a coroutine that hands out each request it starts and is handed
※※ back what the kernel said about it, started in `⎕heap` so that it lasts as
※※ long as `run` needs it
※
※ Nothing runs yet: a task takes its first turn in `run`.  A task may hand over
※ another, which `run` takes on in the round after.
@[export, impure]
fn spawn(s: &mut fs.State, walk: ⌖⇉io.Pending ⇇ io.Answer in ⎕heap):
    s⌖.tasks ← s⌖.tasks ⧺ [fs.Task(.walk ← walk,
                                   .waiting ← io.Pending(.slot ← 0i64 - 1i64, .ring ← 0u64))]

※※ Drive every task handed to `spawn` until each has run to its end.
※※
※※ \param s what the tasks were handed to
※※ \return how many tasks ran to their end
※
※ A round goes through the tasks in order.  One not yet started takes its first
※ turn; one whose request has been answered is resumed with the answer; the rest
※ wait for the next round.  A round in which none could go on waits for the
※ kernel to answer something -- anything -- and that is the only waiting there
※ is.  So the tasks share the one thread, each going on as soon as what it waits
※ for has come and none holding up the others: what `asyncio.run` does for
※ Python's coroutines and an executor for Rust's futures, over the ring `s` keeps.
@[export, impure]
fn run(s: &mut fs.State) → u64:
    let ended: mut u64 = 0u64
    while #s⌖.tasks ≠ 0:
        let now: [fs.Task] = s⌖.tasks
        s⌖.tasks ← []
        let moved: mut bool = false
        foreach one: fs.Task = now:
            let first: bool = one.waiting.slot < 0i64
            if first ∨ io.Pending.ready(one.waiting):
                moved ← true
                let got: i64 = if first: 0i64 else: io.Pending.wait(one.waiting)
                ※ what a read into kept room put there, handed to the task with
                ※ the answer -- now, before anything can take the slot again
                let at: u64 = if first: 0u64 else: io.Pending.taken(one.waiting)
                match ⎕resume(one.walk, io.Answer(.got ← got, .at ← at)):
                    io.Pending(p):
                        s⌖.tasks ← s⌖.tasks ⧺ [fs.Task(.walk ← one.walk, .waiting ← p)]
                    ⊥:
                        ended ← ended + 1u64
            else:
                s⌖.tasks ← s⌖.tasks ⧺ [one]
        if ¬moved:
            fs.State.step(s)
    ended

※※ Read from a device into a run of bytes.
※※
※※ \param from the device to read from
※※ \param into where to put what comes, and how much to ask for: as many bytes
※※ as it holds
※※ \return how many bytes came, and nought where there is no more to come
※※ \raises the number the kernel gave
※
※ There is no form of this that does not wait: what a read is for is the bytes,
※ and a program that has not waited does not have them.  The kernel writes a run
※ of bytes, so into a view that is not one this reads into a copy, copies what
※ came into the view, and the copy goes with the arena it was made in.
@[export, impure]
fn read(from: &mut io.Reader, into: mut u8⟦⟧) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    let scratch: mut arena = ⎕arena
    let laid: u8⟦⟧ = ⎕one_run(into) in scratch
    let at: u64 = if #laid = 0: 0u64 else: ⎕address(&laid⟦0⟧)
    let got: u64 ¤size = flush(io.Reader.start(from, at, ⎕drop(#laid))?)?
    if #laid ≠ 0 and ⎕address(&laid⟦0⟧) ≠ ⎕address(&into⟦0⟧):
        into ← laid
    got

※※ Walk what `from` holds a line at a time.
※※
※※ \param from the device to read
※※ \return each line with the end of line after it -- the last one without,
※※ where the device ends without one -- or the number the kernel gave, which
※※ ends the walk
※
※ What each turn gives is a view into a buffer the walk keeps, good until the
※ next turn: nothing is copied and nothing allocated, which is what C's
※ `getline` into one buffer does.  The end of line is kept, as `getline` and
※ Python's walk of a file keep it, so that a reader can tell a last line that
※ had one from one that did not -- which writing the text back out, as `cat`
※ does, has to.  A reader that wants to keep a line copies it.
※
※ The longest line it hands out is 1024 bytes, the buffer's length: a longer one
※ is refused with `ENOBUFS` rather than handed out in pieces a reader would take
※ for lines.  The buffer is in the walk's frame, which is why it is not larger: a
※ frame is the caller's room or the walk's description.
@[export]
fn read_lines(from: &mut io.Reader) ⇉ u8⟦⟧ ? i32:
    let buf: mut u8⟦1024⟧ = 1024 ⍴ 0u8
    let start: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    let end: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    let more: mut bool = true
    while more:
        let at: mut u64 ¤idx = start
        ※ `and` and not `∧`: at the end of what was read there is nothing to look at
        while at < end and buf⟦at⟧ ≠ 10u8:
            at ← at + 1
        if at < end:
            yield buf⟦start…at + 1⟧
            start ← at + 1
        else:
            if start > 0:
                ※ what is left of the last line goes to the front
                let left: u64 ¤idx = end - start
                let i: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
                while i < left:
                    buf⟦i⟧ ← buf⟦start + i⟧
                    i ← i + 1
                start ← 0
                end ← left
            if end = 1024:
                yield ⊥ 105i32
                more ← false
            else:
                match read(from, buf⟦end…1024⟧):
                    u64 ¤size(n):
                        if n = 0:
                            if end > start:
                                yield buf⟦start…end⟧
                            more ← false
                        else:
                            end ← end + ⎕unit(⎕drop(n), ⌜idx⌝)
                    ⊥(e):
                        yield ⊥ e
                        more ← false

※ -- writing text ------------------------------------------------------------

※※ Write `total` bytes from `at` to `to`, offering what is left until none is.
※
※ The kernel may take fewer bytes than it was offered -- a pipe with room for
※ some of them, a signal part way -- and a program that printed a line wants the
※ line printed.  So this is the loop `write_sync` does not do, and it is here
※ rather than in each caller because both of the two want it.
fn Io.all_of(to: &mut io.Writer, at: u64, total: u64) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    let sent: mut u64 = 0u64
    while sent < total:
        let went: u64 = ⎕drop(flush(io.Writer.start(to, at + sent, total - sent)?)?)
        if went = 0u64:
            ※ No progress and no refusal: nothing more will happen, and saying so
            ※ beats asking for ever.  `EIO` is what the kernel would have said.
            return ⊥ 5i32
        sent ← sent + went
    ⎕unit(sent, ⌜size⌝)

※※ Write every byte of `what` to `to`, answering how many went.
※※
※※ \param to the device to write to
※※ \param what the bytes to write
※※ \return how many bytes went, which is all of them
※※ \raises the number the kernel gave
@[export]
fn Io.print(to: &mut io.Writer, what: u8⟦⟧) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    let total: u64 = ⎕drop(#what)
    if total = 0u64:
        ⎕unit(0u64, ⌜size⌝)
    else:
        ※ the kernel reads a run of bytes: a view that is not one is copied, and
        ※ the copy goes with its arena once the kernel has it all
        let scratch: mut arena = ⎕arena
        let laid: u8⟦⟧ = ⎕one_run(what) in scratch
        Io.all_of(to, ⎕address(&laid⟦0⟧), total)

※※ The same, with a newline after it, written as one request.
※※
※※ \details One request and not two: `writev` takes a list of runs of bytes, so
※※ the text and the newline reach the kernel together and nothing else writing to
※※ the same device can land between them.  Where the kernel takes fewer bytes than
※※ it was offered the rest is written plainly, which is simpler than walking the
※※ list and is the uncommon case.
※※
※※ \param to the device to write to
※※ \param what the bytes to write before the newline
※※ \return how many bytes went, which is `#what` and one more
※※ \raises the number the kernel gave
@[export]
fn Io.println(to: &mut io.Writer, what: u8⟦⟧) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    ※ in the frame and not in the image: nothing here reaches past what it was handed
    let newline: mut u8⟦1⟧ = ⟦10u8⟧
    let text: u64 = ⎕drop(#what)
    let after: u64 = ⎕address(&newline⟦0⟧)
    if text = 0u64:
        Io.all_of(to, after, 1u64)
    else:
        ※ the kernel reads runs of bytes: a view that is not one is copied, and
        ※ the copy goes with its arena once the kernel has it all
        let scratch: mut arena = ⎕arena
        let laid: u8⟦⟧ = ⎕one_run(what) in scratch
        let before: u64 = ⎕address(&laid⟦0⟧)
        ※ The kernel's `iovec` twice over: where the bytes are and how many.
        let parts: mut u64⟦4⟧ = ⟦before, text, after, 1u64⟧
        let went: u64 = ⎕drop(flush(io.Writer.start_several(to, ⎕address(&parts⟦0⟧), 2u64)?)?)
        let total: u64 = text + 1u64
        if went = total:
            ⎕unit(total, ⌜size⌝)
        elif went < text:
            ⎕drop(Io.all_of(to, before + went, text - went)?)
            Io.all_of(to, after, 1u64)
        else:
            Io.all_of(to, after + (went - text), total - went)

※ -- the text of a value -----------------------------------------------------

※※ The text of a value, in one definition for every type.
※※
※※ \details One definition and not one per type: the language has no overloading,
※※ so a glyph has one meaning per number of operands -- and a generic body with
※※ `comptime if` is what says a different thing for each type inside that one
※※ meaning.  An arm not taken is not checked, which is what lets the arms be of
※※ different shapes.
※※
※※ A record is written by reflection over its fields, so a type this module was
※※ never told about is written without being told: `⎕isrecord` picks the arm,
※※ `⎕typename` is the name in front, and `⎕fields` is the fields as a tuple of
※※ pairs, which `comptime foreach` walks.  It recurses, so a field that is a
※※ record is written the same way.
※※
※※ A number is written in *base*, ten unless said otherwise and anything from two
※※ to thirty-six, the digits past nine being letters -- small ones, or capitals
※※ where *upper* says so: `std.text(x, .base ← 16u8)`.  Either is ignored for a
※※ value that is not a number.  The arena is the one the call stands in where it
※※ names none, which in a template's hole is the template's own.
※※
※※ \param v what to write
※※ \param a the arena the text is made in
※※ \param base how many digits a place of a number has
※※ \param upper whether the digits past nine are capitals
※※ \return what a program would have written to mean it
@[export]
fn text(v: T', a: &mut arena, base: u8 ← 10u8, upper: bool ← false) → str in a, ⎕static
        pre(base ≥ 2u8 ∧ base ≤ 36u8):
    comptime if ⎕typeof(⌜v⌝) = ⌜str⌝:
        ※ Copied, so that what comes back is made in `a` as the signature says:
        ※ the text handed over may be made in an arena that goes first.
        "" ⧺ v in a
    comptime elif ⎕typeof(⌜v⌝) = ⌜bool⌝:
        if v: "true" else: "false"
    comptime elif ⎕typeof(⌜v⌝) = ⌜char⌝:
        ⎕str(v, a)
    comptime elif ⎕isrecord(⌜T'⌝):
        ※ The notation the language itself uses for a record, which is also what
        ※ reads back as the same value.
        let opened: str = ⎕typename(⌜T'⌝) ⧺ "(" in a
        let out: mut str = opened
        comptime foreach f := ⎕fields(v):
            if out ≠ opened:
                out ← out ⧺ ", " in a
            let said: str = text(f⟦1⟧, a)
            out ← out ⧺ "." ⧺ f⟦0⟧ ⧺ " ← " ⧺ said in a
        out ⧺ ")" in a
    else:
        ※ A number, one place at a time and least significant first, so nothing
        ※ has to know how many places there are.
        ※
        ※ Nothing here names a width: the type it is compiled for settles what the
        ※ arithmetic is, so this one body is every integer type the language has.
        ※ And nothing is ever negated -- the remainder of a negative number is
        ※ negative and its digit is what that is away from nought, which is the one
        ※ way to write the smallest value a type has, there being no positive of it.
        let zero: T' = ⎕narrow(0u8, ⌜T'⌝) ?? v
        let radix: T' = ⎕narrow(base, ⌜T'⌝) ?? v
        let below: bool = v < zero
        let out: mut str = ""
        let rest: mut T' = v
        while §places true:
            let d: T' = rest % radix ?? zero
            let away: T' = if below: zero - d else: d
            let place: u64 = ⎕narrow(away, ⌜u64⌝) ?? 0u64
            ※ A digit past nine is a letter, counted on from the one before `a`.
            let past: u64 = if upper: 55u64 else: 87u64
            let code: u64 = place + (if place < 10u64: 48u64 else: past)
            let c: char = ⎕chr(⎕narrow(code, ⌜u32⌝) ?? 48u32)
            out ← ⎕str(c, a) ⧺ out in a
            rest ← rest ÷ radix ?? zero
            if rest = zero:
                break §places
        ※ The raised minus the language writes a negative literal with, so that what
        ※ comes out reads back as what went in.
        if below: "⁻" ⧺ out in a else: out

※※ Where a value's text stands in a field wider than it.
※※
※※ \details `natural` is what the value is: a number on the right and anything else
※※ on the left, which is what lines up a column of each.
@[export]
enum Align:
    natural ;
    left ;
    right ;
    centre

※※ The text of a value in a field at least *width* characters wide.
※※
※※ \details What is left over is *fill*, on whichever side *align* says; centred,
※※ the odd one goes on the right.  A width counts characters, as `#` of text does,
※※ so a character of several bytes is one -- and one that a terminal shows two
※※ columns wide is one as well, which nothing here can know.  Text wider than the
※※ field is all of it.  A field filled with noughts on the right of a number keeps
※※ the sign in front of them, `⁻0042`, which is the only way a number padded so
※※ reads as one; any text that begins with a sign is taken that way.
※※
※※ So `std.padded(x, .width ← 8u64)` is a number in a column, and
※※ `std.padded(std.text(x, .base ← 16u8), .width ← 4u64, .fill ← '0',
※※ .align ← std.Align.right)` four hexadecimal digits.
※※
※※ \param v what to write; text is itself
※※ \param a the arena the text is made in
※※ \param width how many characters the field is at least
※※ \param fill what the rest of the field is made of
※※ \param align where the value goes in the field
※※ \return the field
@[export]
fn padded(v: T', a: &mut arena, width: u64 ← 0u64, fill: char ← ' ',
          align: Align ← Align.natural) → str in a, ⎕static:
    let said: str = text(v, a)
    let count: u64 = ⎕drop(#said)
    if count ≥ width:
        return said
    let way: mut Align = align
    if way = Align.natural:
        comptime if ⎕typeof(⌜v⌝) = ⌜str⌝:
            way ← Align.left
        comptime elif ⎕typeof(⌜v⌝) = ⌜bool⌝:
            way ← Align.left
        comptime elif ⎕typeof(⌜v⌝) = ⌜char⌝:
            way ← Align.left
        comptime elif ⎕isrecord(⌜T'⌝):
            way ← Align.left
        else:
            way ← Align.right
    let gap: u64 = width - count
    let before: mut u64 = gap ÷ 2u64 ?? 0u64
    if way = Align.left:
        before ← 0u64
    elif way = Align.right:
        before ← gap
    let one: str = ⎕str(fill, a)
    let ahead: mut str = ""
    let n: mut u64 = 0u64
    while n < before:
        ahead ← ahead ⧺ one in a
        n ← n + 1u64
    let behind: mut str = ""
    while n < gap:
        behind ← behind ⧺ one in a
        n ← n + 1u64
    if fill = '0' and before ≠ 0u64:
        ※ Noughts go after a sign, which stays in front.
        let first: mut bool = true
        let sign: mut str = ""
        let rest: mut str = ""
        foreach c: char = said:
            if first and (c = '⁻' or c = '+'):
                sign ← ⎕str(c, a)
            else:
                rest ← rest ⧺ ⎕str(c, a) in a
            first ← false
        if sign ≠ "":
            return sign ⧺ ahead ⧺ rest ⧺ behind in a
    ahead ⧺ said ⧺ behind in a

※※ The same, written where a reader expects an operator.
※※
※※ \details APL's own glyph for this: monadic `⍕` formats a value.  A program that
※※ wants something else for a type of its own writes the definition for its own
※※ file, which wins over this one, tests for the types it cares about, and hands
※※ the rest back to `text` -- which is why both are here.
※※
※※ \param v what to write
※※ \return what a program would have written to mean it
@[export]
fn `⍕`(v: T', a: &mut arena) → str in a, ⎕static:
    text(v, a)

※※ The same with no arena named, which is the one the compiler provides.
※※
※※ \details Two definitions of one glyph, told apart by how many operands they take,
※※ which is how every other operator with two readings is told apart.  This one takes
※※ room from `⎕heap`, which a pure function may do -- what it makes is new -- and
※※ `⍕v in a` is how a caller says to take it from another arena.
※※
※※ \param v what to write
※※ \return what a program would have written to mean it
@[export]
fn `⍕`(v: T') → str in ⎕heap, ⎕static:
    text(v, &mut ⎕heap)

※ -- formatting ---------------------------------------------------------------

※※ The pieces so far with one more after them, joining no more than it has to.
※
※ What this is for is that a template with no holes should come out as the literal it
※ is, and not as a join of that literal with nothing: a join takes room that outlives
※ the call, so a `print` of plain text would otherwise ask its caller for permission to
※ allocate in order to write something it already had.
@[impure]
comptime fn after(answer: syntax, started: bool, run: str, piece: syntax,
                  where: syntax) → syntax:
    if ¬started:
        if #run = 0:
            piece
        else:
            ⌜$(run) ⧺ $piece in $where⌝
    elif #run = 0:
        ⌜$answer ⧺ $piece in $where⌝
    else:
        ⌜$answer ⧺ $(run) ⧺ $piece in $where⌝

※※ The piece of the program a template and its arguments come to.
※※
※※ \details A `comptime fn` and not a macro, because more than one macro wants it: a
※※ macro is one function and cannot be two, and this is the one thing `format`,
※※ `print` and `println` all do.  What it answers is a join of the pieces between the
※※ holes and `⍕` of each argument.
※※
※※ `{name}` is a hole too, and takes no argument: it is `⍕` of what *name* means
※※ where the template was written, which is the one name a macro reads at its
※※ caller.  Only a name -- an expression goes in as an argument to a `{}`.
※※
※※ \param template the text, with `{}` where an argument goes and `{name}` where
※※ what a name means does
※※ \param args the arguments, as the one piece several of them are
※※ \param where the arena the joins take room from
※※ \param skipped how many of the pieces of *args* come before the arguments
※※ \return the piece the two come to
@[impure]
comptime fn formatted(template: syntax, args: syntax,
                      where: syntax, skipped: u64) → syntax:
    let said: str = ⎕name(template)
    let holes: u64 = ⎕parts(args) - skipped
    let answer: mut syntax = ⌜""⌝
    let started: mut bool = false
    let run: mut str = ""
    let taken: mut u64 = 0u64
    ※ What the last character left behind: nothing, an opening brace, a closing one,
    ※ or a name begun inside braces.
    let after: mut u8 = 0u8
    let name: mut str = ""
    foreach c: char = said:
        if after = 3u8:
            if c = '}':
                ※ A name, which means what it means where the template was written:
                ※ the one name a macro reads at its caller.
                let named: syntax = ⎕caller_name(name)
                answer ← after(answer, started, run,
                               ⌜⍕$named in $where⌝, where)
                started ← true
                run ← ""
                after ← 0u8
            else:
                name ← name ⧺ ⎕str(c)
        elif after = 1u8:
            after ← 0u8
            if c = '{':
                ※ Two braces are one brace, which is how a template says a brace.
                run ← run ⧺ "{"
            elif c = '}':
                if taken ≥ holes:
                    return ⎕refuse("the template has more holes than there are arguments")
                let next: syntax = ⎕part(args, taken + skipped)
                answer ← after(answer, started, run,
                               ⌜⍕$next in $where⌝, where)
                started ← true
                run ← ""
                taken ← taken + 1u64
            else:
                name ← ⎕str(c)
                after ← 3u8
        elif after = 2u8:
            after ← 0u8
            if c = '}':
                run ← run ⧺ "}"
            else:
                return ⎕refuse("a '}' on its own, which a template writes as '}}'")
        elif c = '{':
            after ← 1u8
        elif c = '}':
            after ← 2u8
        else:
            run ← run ⧺ ⎕str(c)
    if after ≠ 0u8:
        return ⎕refuse("the template ends in the middle of a brace")
    if taken < holes:
        return ⎕refuse("the template has fewer holes than there are arguments")
    if ¬started:
        ※ No holes at all: the template is the literal, and a join of it with nothing
        ※ would be a request for room that nothing needs.
        ⌜$(run)⌝
    elif #run = 0:
        answer
    else:
        ⌜$answer ⧺ $(run) in $where⌝

※※ The text a template and its arguments come to.
※※
※※ \details A macro and not a function: the template is handed over as it is written,
※※ which is what lets the holes in it be counted against the arguments before the
※※ program runs.  `{}` takes the next argument, `{name}` is what *name* means where the
※※ template was written, `{{` and `}}` are braces that stand for themselves, and a template that does not agree with its arguments is refused at the
※※ invocation in words of this macro's own.
※※
※※ What it answers is a join of the pieces between the holes and `⍕` of each argument,
※※ so nothing happens while the program runs that would not have happened had the join
※※ been written out -- and `⍕` is what a program may say its own type's text with.
※※
※※ The joins it writes take room from `⎕heap`, so what it answers lasts as long as the
※※ program and a pure function may answer it -- or from an arena named before the
※※ template, `std.format⌜scratch, "{} and {}", a, b⌝`, so that what it answers is made
※※ there and goes when the arena is given back.  Which of the two is asked of the first
※※ piece: a template is text written out, and anything else names the arena.
※※
※※ \param first the template, with `{}` where an argument goes; or the arena, and the
※※ template the first of the rest
※※ \param args what the holes stand for
※※ \return the text they come to
@[export, impure]
macro format(first: syntax, ⁂args: syntax) → syntax:
    if ⎕name(⎕head(first)) = "str":
        formatted(first, args, ⌜⎕heap⌝, 0u64)
    elif ⎕parts(args) = 0u64:
        ⎕refuse("an arena is named, and no template follows it")
    else:
        formatted(⎕part(args, 0u64), args, first, 1u64)

※※ Write a template and its arguments to a device, and answer how many bytes went.
※※
※※ \details A macro, because `format` is one: the template is handed over as it is
※※ written so that its holes can be counted against the arguments before the program
※※ runs.  What it expands to is the join `format` would have answered, handed to
※※ `Io.print` -- so nothing is buffered and what reaches the device is one request.
※※
※※ The text is built in a **pool** of the expansion's own: room is taken from it for as
※※ long as the line is being built, and the whole of it goes back at once when the block
※※ the invocation stands in is left, after the write has answered.  A pool of every
※※ invocation's own, rather than one the module keeps, is what makes giving it back
※※ safe: nothing else can be holding text in it, and an argument that itself prints
※※ builds its line in a pool of its own.
※※
※※ \param to the device to write to
※※ \param template the text, with `{}` where an argument goes
※※ \param args what the holes stand for
※※ \return how many bytes went
@[export, impure]
macro print(to: syntax, template: syntax, ⁂args: syntax) → syntax:
    let said: syntax = formatted(template, args, ⌜pool⌝, 0u64)
    ⌜
        let pool: mut arena = ⎕arena
        Io.print($to, ⎕bytes($said))
    ⌝

※※ The same, with a newline after it, written as one request.
※※
※※ \details The newline goes to the kernel in the same request as the text, `Io.println`
※※ being what does that -- so a line cannot be split by something else writing to the
※※ same device.
※※
※※ \param to the device to write to
※※ \param template the text, with `{}` where an argument goes
※※ \param args what the holes stand for
※※ \return how many bytes went, which is one more than the text
@[export, impure]
macro println(to: syntax, template: syntax, ⁂args: syntax) → syntax:
    let said: syntax = formatted(template, args, ⌜pool⌝, 0u64)
    ⌜
        let pool: mut arena = ⎕arena
        Io.println($to, ⎕bytes($said))
    ⌝

※ -- describing a build --------------------------------------------------------

※※ What a build function is handed, and what it writes into to say what to build.
※※
※※ \details A function marked `@[build]` is run by the compiler rather than
※※ compiled into anything, and what it leaves in this object is what gets built.
※※ The fields are the settings a command line would otherwise carry; the run of
※※ things to build is added to with `add_executable`, there being no way to write
※※ a growing list of them in the language yet.
※※
※※ \note A field left alone keeps what the command line said, so a build file
※※ says only what it means to decide.
@[export]
type Build =
    ※ Where what is built is written.  A relative name is relative to where the
    ※ compiler was run, as every other name on a command line is.
    output_dir : str ;
    ※ What to build for, written the way `--target` writes it.
    target : str ;
    ※ How hard to work on the code, which is what `-O` says.
    opt_level : u8 ;
    ※ Which of the target's microarchitecture levels may be used, or nothing
    ※ where the target's default is wanted.
    mclevel : str ;
    ※ How much room a program's own stack has, and how much unreachable space
    ※ sits below it.
    stack_size : u64 ;
    guard_size : u64 ;
    ※ What the compiler was run with, read the way a program reads its own.
    ※ Nothing may be put in it: a build reads the environment and does not
    ※ make one.
    env : ⸨str: str⸩

※※ Add a program to what this build builds.
※※
※※ \param b what the build is described in
※※ \param name what the program is called, which is also what the file is called
※※ \param sources the files it is built from, of which there must be at least one
@[export, builtin]
fn add_executable(b: &mut Build, name: str, sources: str⟦⟧)

※※ What the command line said about a name, or what the build file chose.
※※
※※ \details `-Dname=value` on the command line is how a build is told something
※※ from outside; this is how the build function reads it.  A name the command
※※ line said nothing about answers with the fallback, so a build file always has
※※ an answer and never has to ask whether there was one.
※※
※※ \param b what the build is described in
※※ \param name what to look for, which is what `-D` writes before the `=`
※※ \param fallback what to answer with where the command line said nothing
※※ \return what was said, or the fallback
@[export, builtin]
fn option(b: &mut Build, name: str, fallback: str) → str

※※ The same question, asked of something that is either so or not.
※※
※※ \details `-Dname` on its own means it is so; `-Dname=` takes `true`, `yes`,
※※ `on` and `1` for so and anything else for not.
※※
※※ \param b what the build is described in
※※ \param name what to look for
※※ \param fallback what to answer with where the command line said nothing
※※ \return whether it is so
@[export, builtin]
fn option_flag(b: &mut Build, name: str, fallback: bool) → bool
